  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Rain returns Thursday as colder temperatures \nfollow Friday into the weekend, with mountain snow and brisk \nconditions.\n\nDry and warm conditions are going to continue through Wednesday as \nupper troughing pulls offshore and weak ridging quickly traverses \nthe region. Another chilly night ahead as skies remain clear and \nwinds are calm across most of the area. Lows tonight settle in the \nmid 30s to lower 40s. A bit more cloud cover tomorrow keeps highs in \nthe mid to upper 60s, but that is still above normal for this time \nof year. \n\nA low pressure system passing through the Ohio River Valley and into \nthe Mid-Atlantic brings scattered showers across our region beginning \nThursday morning across the Alleghenies. Showers are expected to \nmove eastward across the area throughout the day. Depending on how \nfast this system moves offshore, precipitation could linger into \nFriday although primarily confined to the Allegheny Mountains and \neast of I-95.\n\nThe upper trough across the Northeast to OH Valley to Mid-Atlantic \ncuts off Friday, meanders south a bit on Saturday, then quickly \nflattens out Sunday into early next week as strong Canadian High \npressure builds in from the west. Behind Thursday's cold front, \ntemperatures are forecasted to be 5-10 degrees below mid November \nnormals. Reinforcing fronts are likely to cross the area through the \nweekend, maintaining cold temperatures and brisk winds. Combined \nwith ample cloud cover this brings us the season's first noticeable \ncold period.\n\nThe Allegheny Mountains are likely see their first accumulating snow \nof the season Friday into Saturday. A deepening low off the southern \ncoast of New England will produce northwest winds into the \nmountains, with moisture feeding in a favorable direction from the \nGreat Lakes. As temperatures cool Thursday night, rain transitions \nto a rain/snow mix, then likely all snow sometime on Friday. \nMarginal temperatures on Friday prevent much of that from \naccumulating, but that should change Friday night as temps drop to \nthe 20s in the Alleghenies. Elevations above 3000 feet are most \nlikely to see accumulating snow, but the marginal temps during the \ndaytime should keep snow totals on the low end (at least compared to \nwhat some of the model guidance is indicating). As the surface high \nbuilds in on Sunday, drier air should bring an end to snow showers \nby Sunday afternoon.\n\nChilly and brisk conditions continue on Sunday as high pressure \nbuilds to our west. Winds calm next week with temperatures remaining \nbelow normal.",
